{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting — the validator has multiple branches and the error output rotates which branch it shows. This run's message reveals a branch where transition.type must be 'DISSOLVE' | 'SMART_ANIMATE' | 'SCROLL_ANIMATE', with 'matchLayers' required and 'direction' unrecognized.\n\nSo combining both runs:\n- transition type accepts: MOVE_IN | MOVE_OUT | PUSH | SLIDE_IN | SLIDE_OUT (first error) OR DISSOLVE | SMART_ANIMATE | SCROLL_ANIMATE (second error). That's weird — the validator seems inconsistent, or... maybe there are TWO action variants: one for \"Navigate\" actions with MOVE_IN/etc and one for... Hmm.\n\nWait, look at the second error more carefully: the branch with transition says:\n- \"Invalid enum value. Expected 'DISSOLVE' | 'SMART_ANIMATE' | 'SCROLL_ANIMATE', received 'PUSH'\"\n- \"Unrecognized key(s) in object: 'direction'\"\n- \"Required value missing at [0].action.transition.matchLayers\"\n- and earlier in run 1: \"Required value missing at [0].action.transition.easing\" and \"Invalid enum value. Expected 'MOVE_IN' | 'MOVE_OUT' | 'PUSH' | 'SLIDE_IN' | 'SLIDE_OUT', received 'DISSOLVE'\" + \"Required value missing at [0].action.transition.direction\"\n\nHmm, these are contradictory unless there are two transition variants being tried:\nVariant A: {type: DISSOLVE|SMART_ANIMATE|SCROLL_ANIMATE, matchLayers, easing?, duration}\nVariant B: {type: MOVE_IN|MOVE_OUT|PUSH|SLIDE_IN|SLIDE_OUT, direction, easing, duration}\n\nIn run 1 (transition: {type:'DISSOLVE', duration:0.25}), the validator tried variant B and complained type DISSOLVE invalid + direction missing. It may ALSO have tried variant A but the error output only showed a subset? In run 1, the transition branch said: \"Expected [0].action.transition to be one of the following, but none matched: [twice nested] Required easing missing; Invalid enum DISSOLVE vs MOVE_IN...; Expected null, received object\". Hmm only 2 sub-branches shown.\n\nIn run 2 (transition: {type:'PUSH', direction:'LEFT', easing, duration}), validator tried variant A: type PUSH invalid, direction unrecognized, matchLayers missing. The nested structure shows: \"Multiple issues at transition: Invalid enum...; Unrecognized direction; Required matchLayers missing\" then \"Expected null, received object at transition\".\n\nSo validator branches are non-deterministic?? Odd. More likely: the validator tries MULTIPLE action variants, each with its own transition schema, and the error message truncates/rotates which ones it prints.\n\nKey insight: this looks like a Zod-style validator where the NODE action schema might actually want... hmm.\n\nLet me think about what schema would satisfy. Possibility: The transition schema in this environment is the NEW Figma \"transition\" format:\n- For cross-fade/dissolve: {type:'DISSOLVE', duration, easing}\n- For directional: {type:'MOVE_IN', direction, easing, duration}\n\nRun 1 showed the enum MOVE_IN|MOVE_OUT|PUSH|SLIDE_IN|SLIDE_OUT accepted for one variant, requiring direction and easing. Run 2 showed enum DISSOLVE|SMART_ANIMATE|SCROLL_ANIMATE for another variant requiring matchLayers (probably SMART_ANIMATE requires matchLayers; DISSOLVE requires easing only?).\n\nThe problem: when I pass type PUSH + direction + easing, the validator apparently only reported the DISSOLVE-branch failure... and maybe the MOVE_IN-branch would have accepted it? But it said \"none matched\". So with PUSH+direction+easing+duration, the MOVE_IN branch should match... unless that branch also requires something else, like 'matchLayers'? or maybe the branch requires navigation to be something else, or the easing type 'EASE_IN_AND_OUT' is invalid, causing the whole branch to fail and the error output shows only the other branch's issues.\n\nActually the error output IS confusing because it prints nested \"Expected transition to be one of the following, but none matched\" twice. In run 1, the transition part showed two sub-branches: one requiring easing (probably DISSOLVE branch: {type DISSOLVE, easing required}), one with enum MOVE_IN... requiring direction. So transition variants:\nT1: {type: 'DISSOLVE'|'SMART_ANIMATE'|'SCROLL_ANIMATE', matchLayers, easing, duration?} — in run1 with type DISSOLVE: complained \"Required easing missing\" (not type invalid) → T1 accepts DISSOLVE and REQUIRES easing! And in run 2 with type PUSH: complained type invalid + direction unrecognized + matchLayers missing → T1 requires matchLayers (hmm, even for DISSOLVE? maybe matchLayers optional but reported because... no, \"Required value missing at matchLayers\" was listed).\n\nHmm wait, in run 1, did it complain about matchLayers missing for DISSOLVE? The run-1 transition section: \"Required value missing at [0].action.transition.easing; Multiple issues: Invalid enum expected MOVE_IN|MOVE_OUT|PUSH|SLIDE_IN|SLIDE_OUT received DISSOLVE; Required value missing at direction; ... Expected null received object\". I don't see matchLayers in run 1. And no complaint that 'duration' was unrecognized. So T1 = {type DISSOLVE|SMART_ANIMATE|SCROLL_ANIMATE, easing (required), matchLayers (maybe optional), duration?}.\n\nT2 = {type MOVE_IN|MOVE_OUT|PUSH|SLIDE_IN|SLIDE_OUT, direction (required), easing (required), duration?}.\n\nIn run 2, I passed type PUSH, direction LEFT, easing {type EASE_IN_AND_OUT}, duration 0.3. T2 should have matched! Unless easing {type:'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'} is invalid in T2's easing schema, and the validator only printed T1's complaints... The run-2 output shows only ONE transition sub-branch (the DISSOLVE one). Possibly the output truncated the T2 branch, or T2 failed on easing.\n\nHmm wait, actually re-reading run 2's transition section:\n\n\"Expected [0].action.transition to be one of the following, but none matched:\n  Expected [0].action.transition to be one of the following, but none matched:\n    Multiple issues at [0].action.transition:\n      Invalid enum value. Expected 'DISSOLVE' | 'SMART_ANIMATE' | 'SCROLL_ANIMATE', received 'PUSH' at [0].action.transition.type\n      Unrecognized key(s) in object: 'direction' at [0].action.transition\n    Required value missing at [0].action.transition.matchLayers\n  Expected null, received object at [0].action.transition\"\n\nOnly the DISSOLVE branch appears. In run 1, only the MOVE_IN-ish branch appeared. This is bizarre — like the validator message is unstable (maybe it reports a random/failing subset). OR maybe the schema for transition is a UNION and the error printer only shows some union members.\n\nStrategy: make the transition satisfy BOTH possible interpretations as much as possible. For a PUSH-style navigation: type 'PUSH' requires direction + easing. Let me try easing with proper Figma easing format. Maybe easing must be like {type:'EASE_OUT'}? Or maybe the newer format is {easingFunction: {...}}? Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: try type 'DISSOLVE' with easing {type:'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'} and matchLayers [] and duration 0.25, no direction:\n\ntransition: {type:'DISSOLVE', easing:{type:'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'}, matchLayers: [], duration: 0.25}\n\nIf T1 requires easing only (+ maybe matchLayers), this matches. For the sheet, use MOVE_IN/MOVE_OUT with direction+easing:\n\ntransition: {type:'MOVE_IN', direction:'BOTTOM', easing:{type:'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'}, duration:0.3}\n\nBut run 2 showed PUSH+direction+easing FAILED. Why? If T2 exists and requires exactly {type, direction, easing}, my object should match. Unless T2's easing schema rejects {type:'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'}. Maybe easing must be an enum STRING like easing: 'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'? Or an object {easingFunction:'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'}? Hmm.\n\nIn the real current Figma Plugin API (2024+), transitions are like:\n{type: 'DISSOLVE', duration: 0.2, easing: {type: 'EASE_IN_AND_OUT_AND_BACK'? no...}}\n\nReal API: Transition = DISSOLVE | SMART_ANIMATE | MOVE_IN | MOVE_OUT | PUSH | SLIDE_IN | SLIDE_OUT, each with easing: Easing and duration: number. Easing = {type: 'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'} | {type:'EASE_IN'} | ... | {type:'GENTLE'|'QUICK'|'BOUNCY'|'SLOW', easingFunction?...} Hmm actually newer Figma added easing types like 'GENTLE' etc. The base: EasingFunction = 'LINEAR'|'EASE_IN'|'EASE_OUT'|'EASE_IN_AND_OUT', and Easing = {type: EasingFunction} | {type:'GENTLE', ...}? I think Easing = {type: 'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'} is valid real API.\n\nBut this validator seems custom (this MCP's own validation, \"in set_reactions\"). The error messages come from a zod-ish schema in the MCP server, not Figma itself. And the union reporting is flaky.\n\nLet me try a different easing format and see. Actually, better approach: bisect with tiny tests that fail fast but produce SHORT errors. The full error is huge (~40KB) — that's fine for one test, it's just context tokens. But each test costs ~10-12KB of context. Let me be smart.\n\nTest: transition {type:'DISSOLVE', easing:{type:'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'}, duration:0.25} (no direction, no matchLayers). If the DISSOLVE branch only needs easing, this passes.","signature":"344f9dab676249eea3fcece5"}],"id":"46c68eb5-8c74-44f5-9160-da7068a16689","model":"glm-5.3-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"259a9f97-bab6-42ed-a1f7-e48e66488de3","uuid":"bb0510d7-bcf8-466c-a774-6717a1ea7b19","timestamp":"2026-10-31T22:52:33.747Z","thinking_duration_ms":40427}
